title: From a light back to a number
mode: draw
chapter: Choosing
check: behaves
parts: and or not
---- intro
The decoder turned a number into one light. An **encoder** goes back: one of the four switches `d0` to `d3` is on, and the two lights `a1 a0` show **which** one, as a number.

| on | a1 | a0 |
|----|----|----|
| `d0` | 0 | 0 |
| `d1` | 0 | 1 |
| `d2` | 1 | 0 |
| `d3` | 1 | 1 |

Read the columns instead of the rows: `a0` is 1 for `d1` and for `d3`. Turn on only one switch at a time when you try it.
---- task
Light `a1 a0` with the number of the switch that is on (only one at a time).
---- start
input d0 0 0
input d1 0 60
input d2 0 120
input d3 0 180
led a1 360 40
led a0 360 140
---- reference
input d0, d1, d2, d3
led a1, a0
a1 = d2 | d3
a0 = d1 | d3
---- answer
input d0 0 0
input d1 0 60
input d2 0 120
input d3 0 180
or o1 200 40
or o0 200 140
led a1 360 40
led a0 360 140
d2 -> o1.0
d3 -> o1.1
d1 -> o0.0
d3 -> o0.1
o1 -> a1
o0 -> a0
---- hint1
`a0` is 1 when `d1` or `d3` is on. Which switches make `a1` 1?
---- hint2
Two OR gates: `d1` OR `d3` into `a0`, and `d2` OR `d3` into `a1`. `d0` needs no wire at all: it is the number 0.
---- solution
`a0` = `d1` OR `d3`, `a1` = `d2` OR `d3`. Switch `d0` is 0 0, so it needs no gate.
---- done
A keyboard uses an encoder: one key down becomes the number of that key. The trick of reading a table by columns works for any circuit.
